'Modern template control flow</h3>\n\n<p>Since v17, Angular templates use built-in control flow instead of structural directives. Older code uses <code>*ngIf</code> / <code>*ngFor</code>; modern code may use <code>@if</code> / <code>@for</code> / <code>@switch</code> / <code>@defer</code> — recognize both:</p>\n\n<pre><code>@if (items.length) {\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    @for (item of items; track item.id) {\n      &lt;li&gt;{{ item.name }}&lt;/li&gt;\n    } @empty {\n      &lt;li&gt;No items&lt;/li&gt;\n    }\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n}</code></pre>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>@if</code></strong> — conditional render (replaces <code>*ngIf</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong><code>@for</code></strong> — list render with <code>track</code> (replaces <code>*ngFor</code> with "track by"). <code>@empty</code> handles the empty case.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>@switch</code></strong> — multiple-branch render (replaces <code>*ngSwitch</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong><code>@defer</code></strong> — lazily load and render a template block on demand.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The modern Signals API surface</h3>\n\n<p>Beyond the core three, modern Angular (v17–v22) adds signal-based bindings and async resources. Version-specific; verify before relying on any of these in code:</p>\n\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>API</th><th>Purpose</th><th>When it arrived</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><code>input()</code> / <code>input.required()</code></td><td>Signal-based inputs (declarative, can be read in <code>computed</code>)</td><td>v17, <code>input.required()</code> v17.2+</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>output()</code></td><td>Signal-based output / event emitter</td><td>v17.2+</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>model()</code></td><td>Two-way-binding signal (replaces <code>[(ngModel)]</code>-style bindings)</td><td>v17.2+</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>linkedSignal()</code></td><td>Writable signal that resets when its source changes</td><td>v19 (stable v20)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>toSignal()</code> / <code>toObservable()</code></td><td>Bridges between RxJS Observables and Signals</td><td>v16 preview, stable v20</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>untracked()</code></td><td>Read a signal without creating a dependency</td><td>v16+</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>effect()</code> options / <code>DestroyRef</code></td><td>Cleanup and lifecycle integration for effects</td><td>v16+</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>httpResource()</code> / <code>resource()</code> / <code>rxResource()</code></td><td>Signal-based async data fetching; <code>httpResource</code> wraps <code>HttpClient</code></td><td>experimental v20, stable v22</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Dependency injection — why Angular kept classes</h3>\n\n<p>DI is one reason Angular did not move to hooks. Angular\'s DI is class/injector-centric: a service is constructed and its lifetime managed by the injector hierarchy, injected either via constructor or (modern) the <code>inject()</code> function. Hooks-style DI would require a different design — you cannot inject into a function\'s call order the way React does.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Constructor injection</strong> — <code>constructor(private http: HttpClient)</code>; the classic pattern (still works).</li>\n  <li><strong><code>inject()</code></strong> — functional injection usable in field initializers and within injection context; the modern idiom.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>providedIn: \'root\'</code></strong> — a service provided at the root, with no explicit provider list.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hierarchical injectors</strong> — each component can provide its own instance, scoping service lifetime to a subtree.</li>\n  <li><strong>Injection tokens</strong> — identifiers (symbols or class references) that name what you inject.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<blockquote class="callout">\n  <p>DI gives services a clear construction model and makes testing easy (inject a mock). React can approximate DI with context or external stores, but Angular\'s DI is a first-class framework feature — one reason Angular modernized Signals inside classes instead of removing classes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<h3>Forms — where state, validation, and change detection meet</h3>\n\n<p>Enterprise Angular code is full of forms. Recognize all four generations:</p>\n\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Form type</th><th>What you see</th><th>When common</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Template-driven forms</td><td><code>ngModel</code>, <code>#form="ngForm"</code></td><td>Simpler forms, older code</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Reactive forms</td><td><code>FormGroup</code>, <code>FormControl</code>, <code>FormBuilder</code></td><td>Enterprise apps</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Typed forms</td><td>Typed <code>FormControl&lt;string&gt;</code></td><td>Angular 14+</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Signal forms</td><td>Schema-based validation, <code>[formField]</code> binding</td><td>experimental v21, stable v22 — new/modern apps</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n'